WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.470
- [Kim] This is a painting

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.180
of US general Winfield Scott

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.080
entering Mexico City on
September 15th, 1847.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.950
Scott landed with a US naval
fleet several weeks beforehand.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.070
He bombarded the coastal
stronghold of Veracruz

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.020
and then fought his way
inland toward the capital.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.020
Scott actually followed the same route

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.340
that Spanish conquistador
Hernan Cortes took

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.560
more than 300 years earlier.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.330
Winfield Scott's campaign to Mexico City

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.500
was just one of three fronts

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.340
in the two-year-long,
continent-spanning effort

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.870
of the United States to take
Mexican territory by force.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.200
The other two fronts were in
California and New Mexico.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.200
After the two nations made peace

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.610
by signing the Treaty of
Guadalupe Hidalgo in mid 1848,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.250
the United States gained
over a million square miles

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.350
of new territory,

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.220
a landmass larger than
the Louisiana Purchase.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.170
For Mexico, this war was
a catastrophic defeat,

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:03.330
which resulted in the loss of
about 1/3 of its total area.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.440
The Mexican-American War
doesn't really loom large

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.880
in American memory, compared
to the Revolutionary War

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.580
or the Civil War, but it
was a transformative event

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.570
in the history of the United
States and North America.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:19.120
On the scale of national politics,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.130
the war led to political realignment,

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.040
and eventually, the Civil War.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.700
But on a human scale, it
led to transformations

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.990
in the lives of people
who lived in the West

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.410
who went to bed one day in Mexico

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.170
and woke up the next day
in the United States.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.830
National boundaries
shifted under their feet.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.630
For those people, the outcome
of the war meant new laws,

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.380
customs, new friends and enemies,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.680
and even the loss of
rights and privileges.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.810
So let's dive a little deeper
into the causes and effects

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.440
of the Mexican-American War.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.330
The war began in April of 1846.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:00.100
A Mexican cavalry brigade
attacked US forces

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.750
who were under the command
of General Zachary Taylor

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.560
across the Rio Grande River

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.110
from the town of Matamoros, Mexico.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.340
After this attack, President James K. Polk

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.600
sent a war message to Congress.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.390
He fumed that the Mexican
troops had invaded our territory

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.690
and shed American blood on American soil.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:20.750
Now back up a minute.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:21.840
You may be wondering,

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.630
as many keen observers did at the time,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.572
what exactly were US forces doing there

00:02:27.572 --> 00:02:30.290
near Rio Grande River in the first place?

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.900
And the answer to that
reveals the two major causes

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.640
of the war, Texas annexation
and Manifest Destiny.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.960
Let's start by talking
about Texas annexation.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.420
American settlers, many
of whom were slave owners,

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.950
had been moving to Texas since the 1820s,

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.300
when the region was still
controlled by Spain.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:53.780
After Mexican independence,
the country outlawed slavery.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.250
But the American settlers resisted

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.180
the Mexican government's authority.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.400
In 1836, they rebelled and
won independence for Texas.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.410
They requested the United
States annex the new nation

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:08.870
shortly thereafter, but
adding another slave state

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.770
to the Union was politically dangerous

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.310
for the administration at that time.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:17.780
So Texas remained an
independent nation until 1845.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.780
In 1845, Democratic president
James K. Polk took office.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.720
Now Polk was an ardent expansionist.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.070
He was a believer in Manifest Destiny,

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.680
this idea that God
wanted the United States

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.800
to expand across the
North American continent.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.770
Polk wanted to annex Texas,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.420
which his administration
undertook immediately.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.270
He also desperately wanted California,

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:44.560
which was a hub of commerce
on the Pacific Ocean.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.110
This is actually before
gold was discovered there.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.600
So Polk sent a representative
to the Mexican government

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.860
offering to buy California.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.990
But Mexico said California
was not for sale.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.910
Now Polk was determined
to get this territory

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.380
with blood or money.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.120
So he came up with an alternate plan.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.250
The border between Mexico
and Texas was under dispute.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:10.420
So Polk directed General Zachary Taylor

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.100
to go down into this disputed territory

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.060
and provoke hostilities.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.550
And that's exactly what happened

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.110
when the Mexican cavalry
attacked Taylor's forces.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.250
As far as Mexico was concerned,

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.920
Taylor's troops were
invading their country,

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.080
and they had no choice but to defend it.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.830
Despite Polk's war message saying that

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.760
American blood had been
shed on American soil,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.080
many US politicians were
also skeptical about

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.310
who started the war and where.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.000
A young Whig congressman from
Illinois named Abraham Lincoln

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.021
demanded that Polk show him the exact spot

00:04:45.021 --> 00:04:47.440
where American blood had been shed.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.155
The war that ensued was
longer, costlier, and deadlier

00:04:52.155 --> 00:04:54.570
than the US government had estimated,

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.410
which is often the case with wars.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:00.380
At its conclusion, Polk
had achieved his vision

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:01.660
for Manifest Destiny.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.310
In the Treaty of Guadalupe
Hidalgo, which ended the war,

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.770
the United States agreed
to pay Mexico $15 million,

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.740
and in exchange, Mexico
ceded Texas, California,

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.950
and most of the modern-day
Southwest to the United States.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.260
So what were the effects of this war?

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.940
Well, the addition of this
Mexican cession territory

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.890
had far-reaching consequences
for both the United States

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.070
and the residents of the West.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.610
The existing resident of the territory,

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.339
including Mexicans, Native Americans,

00:05:33.339 --> 00:05:36.890
and the descendants of Spanish colonists,

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:39.800
found that life under the
rule of the United States

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.190
could be very different than
under the rule of Mexico.

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:46.500
Where Mexican law had abolished slavery

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.870
and proscribed equality under the law

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.800
for people regardless of color,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.800
the Texas constitution permitted slavery

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.220
and denied civil rights
to non-white residents.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:59.330
For other residents of the territory,

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.890
life didn't change much at all.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.310
Huge swathes of the West
were actually controlled

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.570
by Native American nations,
like the Comanche Empire,

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:09.900
which didn't care whether
the distant government

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.210
who claimed their territory on paper

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:15.740
was located in Mexico
City or in Washington DC.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:17.670
For the United States government,

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.850
the addition of this new territory
was political kryptonite.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.120
Both Northerners and
Southerners were convinced

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.742
that the opposite region was conspiring

00:06:27.742 --> 00:06:31.360
to limit their economic
opportunities in the West.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:35.060
During the war, Congressman
David Wilmot of Pennsylvania

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.640
introduced a resolution in the House

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.350
that would prohibit slavery

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:42.290
in any territory gained from the conflict.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.853
The reaction to the Wilmot Priviso

00:06:44.853 --> 00:06:49.853
showed just how big the
sectional divide in the country

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.570
was becoming, since party
lines broke down entirely.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.470
Northerners, Whig, and Democrat alike

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:58.430
voted for the Wilmot Proviso,

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:02.840
and Southerners, Whig, and
Democrat alike voted against it.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:05.670
Ultimately, the proviso
passed in the House

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.770
was defeated in the Senate.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.560
And then gold was
discovered in California,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.220
just before the end of the war,

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.740
sending hordes of prospectors West

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:18.630
and making statehood for
California an urgent issue

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:20.880
that would soon upset the balance of power

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.700
between free and slave states in Congress.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.500
In other words, we can draw a direct line

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.120
from the Mexican War

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.760
to the breakdown of the
second party system,

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.520
which was replaced by a solidly
Southern Democratic party

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.950
and a new Northern Republican party,

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:38.753
and from there to the Civil War.

